# Batch 8 (2026-10-20): South America, Central Asia, Middle East, Southeast Asia, Pacific, Africa, medieval and modern mysteries
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[
# --- part 1: archaic humans of East Asia, and South America
X('harbin-dragon-man',"Harbin \"Dragon Man\": a skull hidden in a well, and a face for the Denisovans",H,'confirmed',2,-146000,['fossil','uranium-series dating','ancient proteins','ancient DNA'],
 "A nearly complete archaic human skull said to have been found in 1933 by a labourer building a railway bridge over the Songhua River in Harbin, north-east China, during the Japanese occupation. According to the account given to scientists, he hid it in an abandoned well; his family revealed it only in 2018, and it went to the Hebei GEO University. Described in 2021, it is at least about 146,000 years old (late Middle Pleistocene), has a huge braincase (about 1,420 cc), very heavy brow ridges, a broad flat face and large teeth, and was named the type of a new species, Homo longi (\"dragon man\", after Heilongjiang, \"Black Dragon River\"). In 2025 two studies led by Fu Qiaomei recovered proteins from the skull and mitochondrial DNA from dental calculus on its teeth, both pointing to the Denisovans – a group previously known almost only from a finger bone, teeth and genomes.",
 [('Denisovan identity','The 2025 molecular results led many researchers to treat the Harbin skull as the first fairly complete Denisovan cranium, giving a face to a population known from DNA traces in living people, especially in Oceania and East Asia.'),
  ('Naming dispute','Whether the right name is Homo longi, a Denisovan population within another species, or something else is debated; species names for Middle Pleistocene Asian fossils (Dali, Jinniushan, Xiahe) are contested.'),
  ('Find story','The 1933 discovery and well-hiding story comes from family testimony and cannot be independently checked, so the exact find spot is uncertain; geochemical comparison of the skull with Songhua River sediments was used to support it.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.xinn.2021.100132','doi:10.1016/j.cell.2025.05.040','wiki:Harbin_cranium'],'Harbin_cranium',
 dateNote='At least c. 146,000 years old; surfaced 2018, described 2021',
 changed='2010: Denisovans identified from a finger bone genome. 2018: Harbin skull handed to scientists. 2021: named Homo longi. 2025: protein and mitochondrial DNA evidence link it to the Denisovans.'),
X('homo-luzonensis','Homo luzonensis: a small human species on an island in the Philippines',H,'confirmed',2,-134000,['fossil','uranium-series dating','archaeological'],
 "Teeth, finger and toe bones and part of a thigh bone from Callao Cave on Luzon, the Philippines, were assigned in 2019 to a new species, Homo luzonensis. A first toe bone was reported in 2010 as possibly modern human; further finds showed an odd mix of small, fairly modern-looking molars with curved finger and toe bones resembling Australopithecus. The remains were first dated to at least 50,000–67,000 years ago; a 2023 study gave about 134,000 years. Separately, butchered rhinoceros bones and stone tools from Kalinga, Luzon, show hominins were on the island by about 709,000 years ago – which means some early humans crossed open sea to get there. Locally the species is nicknamed \"Ubag\", after a mythical caveman.",
 [('Island dwarfing','Like Homo floresiensis on Flores, H. luzonensis may be a lineage that became small after long isolation on an island; how its ancestors (perhaps Homo erectus or an earlier hominin) crossed the sea is unknown – rafting after storms or tsunamis has been suggested.'),
  ('Not a separate species?','Some researchers argue the few bones are too limited to define a species and could belong to a variable known population; no DNA has been recovered.'),
  ('Folklore echoes (speculative)','Philippine and Indonesian stories of small wild forest people are sometimes linked in popular writing to these island hominins; no evidence connects such stories to populations that died out tens of thousands of years ago.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41586-019-1067-9','doi:10.1038/s41586-018-0072-8','wiki:Homo_luzonensis'],'Homo_luzonensis',
 dateNote='Fossils c. 134,000 (earlier estimate ≥50,000) years ago; hominins on Luzon by c. 709,000 years ago'),
X('chavin','Chavín de Huántar: the oracle temple of underground galleries',H,'confirmed',2,-1200,['archaeological','radiocarbon','iconographic'],
 "A ceremonial centre at 3,180 m in the Andes of north-central Peru, built and rebuilt from about 1200 BCE and used until about 500–400 BCE by the Chavín culture, whose art style spread widely across Peru. Its stone temples are honeycombed with narrow, dark, stone-lined galleries and ventilation and drainage ducts. At a gallery crossing stands the Lanzón, a 4.5 m carved granite blade showing a fanged deity, probably the centre of an oracle. Carved heads on the outer walls show humans turning into fanged, snot-nosed beings, and a relief of a figure holding a San Pedro cactus (a mescaline source) has been read as evidence of ritual drug use. In 2001 excavations led by John Rick found 20 decorated Strombus-shell trumpets (pututus) inside the galleries. UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1985.",
 [('Engineered experience','Rick and others argue the temple was designed to disorient and awe visitors – darkness, rushing water in ducts, conch-trumpet sounds and psychoactive substances – helping a priestly elite build authority; acoustic studies (e.g. at Stanford) have tested how sound travelled in the galleries.'),
  ('Pilgrimage centre vs capital','Chavín is now usually seen as a pilgrimage and oracle centre, not the capital of an empire; how its art style spread so far is still debated.'),
  ('Fringe readings','Popular alternative writers have presented the transforming-head sculptures as non-human beings or the galleries as advanced technology; these readings are speculative and contradicted by the archaeological context.')],
 ['doi:10.1525/ap3a.2005.14.071','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/330|UNESCO: Chavín (Archaeological Site)|heritage body','wiki:Chavín_de_Huántar'],'Chavín_de_Huántar',end=-500),
X('kuelap','Kuélap and the Chachapoyas "cloud warriors"',H,'confirmed',3,500,['archaeological','radiocarbon','historical records'],
 "A walled settlement on a ridge about 3,000 m up above the Utcubamba valley in Amazonas, northern Peru, built by the Chachapoyas culture. Occupation began around the 5th–6th century CE, most of its structures date from about 900–1100, and it was abandoned around 1570 after the Spanish conquest. Limestone walls 10–20 m high enclose about six hectares and over 400 mostly round buildings, decorated with zigzag and rhomboid friezes, entered through funnel-like passages only one person wide. The Inca conquered the Chachapoyas in the late 15th century. A judge from Chachapoyas, Juan Crisóstomo Nieto, rediscovered the site in 1843; part of the wall collapsed in April 2022.",
 [('Fortress or town','Its walls and narrow gates suggest defence, but it was also a densely lived-in town and burial place; the balance is debated.'),
  ('"White" Chachapoyas (unsupported)','Spanish chroniclers described some Chachapoya people as fair-skinned, and some 20th-century writers (e.g. Jacques de Mahieu) proposed Viking or other Old World origins; there is no archaeological or genetic support, and the Chachapoyas are regarded as an indigenous Andean–Amazonian culture.')],
 ['wiki:Kuélap','wiki:Chachapoya_culture'],'Kuélap',end=1570,dateNote='Occupied c. 5th century–c. 1570; main building phase c. 900–1100'),
X('paracas-skulls','The elongated skulls of Paracas',H,'disputed',2,-800,['archaeological','osteological','claimed DNA tests'],
 "The Paracas culture of Peru's south coast (about 800–100 BCE) is famous for its necropolis textiles and for the skulls Julio Tello excavated from shaft tombs (the Paracas Cavernas) in the late 1920s. Most of these skulls were deliberately reshaped in infancy by binding the head with boards or cloth – artificial cranial deformation, practised in many Andean societies and around the world – producing tall or long heads. Paracas skulls also show some of the earliest trepanation (surgical skull opening) in the Americas, with many patients surviving. Mainstream bioarchaeology treats the long skulls as a cultural practice marking identity, status or group membership.",
 [('Non-human or hybrid origin (speculative)','From about 2014, promoters including Brien Foerster publicised preliminary DNA tests said to show unusual or unknown lineages and claimed the skulls had larger volume than human skulls; these results were not published in a peer-reviewed journal and are rejected by specialists, who note contamination risks and that binding changes shape, not brain size.'),
  ('Cultural explanation','Physical anthropologists point to the wear patterns of binding, ceramics depicting shaped heads, and identical practices documented ethnographically, all consistent with ordinary humans.')],
 ['wiki:Paracas_culture','wiki:Artificial_cranial_deformation'],'Artificial_cranial_deformation',end=-100,dateNote='Paracas culture c. 800–100 BCE'),
X('sacsayhuaman','Sacsayhuamán: walls that fit "without a knife blade"',H,'confirmed',2,1440,['archaeological','historical records','experimental archaeology'],
 "A massive complex on a hill above Cusco, the Inca capital, whose three tiers of zigzag terrace walls are built of huge polygonal limestone and andesite blocks fitted so tightly that, it is often said, a knife blade cannot be slid between them; the largest blocks are estimated at well over 100 tonnes. Spanish chroniclers recorded Inca oral history that it was begun under Pachacuti in the 15th century and continued under Topa Inca and Huayna Capac, with thousands of labourers. It was the scene of fierce fighting in Manco Inca's siege of Cusco in 1536, after which the Spanish quarried much of it for buildings in the city. In the 1980s architect Jean-Pierre Protzen showed experimentally that such blocks could be shaped and fitted with hammerstones and repeated trial fitting.",
 [('Older builders (speculative)','Some alternative writers argue the largest walls predate the Inca and were built by a lost civilisation or with lost technology; no dated evidence supports a pre-Inca origin for the megalithic walls, and Inca quarries, ramps and tool marks are documented.'),
  ('Stone-softening legends','Stories of a plant juice that makes rock soft like clay (repeated by Percy Fawcett among others) are folklore; there is no physical or chemical evidence of softened stone at Inca sites.')],
 ['doi:10.2307/990027','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/273|UNESCO: City of Cuzco|heritage body','wiki:Sacsayhuamán'],'Sacsayhuamán',end=1536,
 dateNote='Construction mainly 15th–early 16th century; battle 1536'),
X('khipu','Khipu: Inca knotted cords – accounts, or a lost writing?',H,'disputed',2,1100,['archaeological','ethnohistorical','statistical analysis'],
 "Khipu (quipu) are bundles of cotton or camelid-fibre cords whose knots, colours, positions and plying stored information; precursors go back to the Wari state in the 1st millennium CE, and the Inca empire used them for censuses, tribute and calendars, read by specialists called khipukamayuq. In 1912 Leland Locke showed that many khipu record numbers in a base-10 positional system. Just under 1,400 survive, many destroyed by the Spanish as idolatrous. In 2005 Gary Urton and Carrie Brezine showed hierarchical accounting relationships between khipu from Puruchuco; in 2018 Manny Medrano and Urton matched six colonial-era khipu from the Santa Valley to a Spanish census, linking cord colours and ply to social categories.",
 [('Narrative writing','Spanish chroniclers said some khipu recorded histories, laws and stories. Sabine Hyland (2017) reported an 18th-century narrative khipu from Collata whose community described it as a letter, with an estimated 95 distinct cord-signs – enough for a logosyllabic system – but this is not yet deciphered.'),
  ('Mostly numerical','Other scholars think most khipu were accounting devices plus memory aids for trained readers, not full writing; no general decipherment of non-numerical khipu exists.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.1113426','doi:10.1215/00141801-4260638','doi:10.1086/691682','wiki:Quipu'],'Quipu',end=1600,
 dateNote='Wari precursors 1st millennium CE; Inca use c. 1400–1532, continued into colonial period'),
X('paititi','Paititi: the last refuge of the Inca',M,'myth-legend',2,1572,['historical records','oral tradition','exploration'],
 "Paititi is a legendary lost Inca city or rich land said to lie east of the Andes in the rainforests of south-east Peru, northern Bolivia or north-west Brazil. In Peruvian versions it is the refuge of the culture hero Inkarri, who withdrew to the jungle and will one day return; others tie it to Incas fleeing the Spanish after the fall of Vilcabamba in 1572. Colonial documents from 1570 onward describe expeditions in search of it, and in 2001 a Jesuit report of about 1600 by missionary Andrés López, describing a jungle city rich in gold, was found in Rome (it is third-hand). Modern expeditions – some fatal, like the 1971 Nichols expedition – have found Inca remains, petroglyphs at Pusharo and natural \"pyramids\" at Paratoari, but no lost city.",
 [('Historical kernel','Historians including Martti Pärssinen and Ari Siiriäinen link the legend to real Inca expansion into the Amazon lowlands; a Finnish–Bolivian dig at Las Piedras (2001–2003) found some imperial Inca pottery, but its Inca origin is disputed.'),
  ('Utopian myth','Others treat Paititi like El Dorado – a myth of a hidden kingdom that grew with colonial treasure-seeking and with hopes of Inca return.')],
 ['wiki:Paititi','wiki:Inkarri'],'Paititi'),
X('llullaillaco','The children of Llullaillaco: Inca sacrifice at 6,700 metres',H,'confirmed',2,1500,['archaeological','biochemical','radiological'],
 "On 16 March 1999 Johan Reinhard, Constanza Ceruti and their team found three Inca children buried near the 6,739 m summit of Llullaillaco volcano on the Argentina–Chile border – one of the highest archaeological sites on Earth. A girl of about 13 (the \"Maiden\"), and a younger boy and girl died around 1500 in a capacocha ceremony, in which chosen children were taken to sacred mountains and sacrificed. Freezing, dry conditions preserved organs, hair and clothing. Analysis of the Maiden's hair (Wilson et al., PNAS 2013) showed her diet and coca and alcohol consumption rose sharply over her last year, especially in the final weeks, consistent with ritual preparation and sedation. The younger girl's body was later struck by lightning. They are displayed at the Museum of High Altitude Archaeology in Salta.",
 [('Interpretation','Researchers read the changes as a staged transformation of the children into sacred offerings, linking empire, mountains and state religion; how willing the children or families were is unknowable.'),
  ('Ethics','Indigenous groups and others have criticised displaying the bodies; the museum shows them one at a time under controlled conditions.')],
 ['doi:10.1073/pnas.1305117110','wiki:Children_of_Llullaillaco'],'Children_of_Llullaillaco',dateNote='Died c. 1462–1507; found 1999'),
X('fuente-magna','The Fuente Magna bowl: Sumerian writing at Lake Titicaca?',H,'disputed',3,1950,['artefact','epigraphic claims'],
 "A large stone vessel reportedly found around 1950 by a farmer near Chua on Lake Titicaca, Bolivia, and now in the Museo de Metales Preciosos in La Paz. Its cultural affiliation, date and find context are uncertain. Its inner surface carries a face and engraved marks that some promoters, especially since the 2000s, have claimed are proto-Sumerian or cuneiform writing, implying ancient Mesopotamian contact with South America. Archaeologist Alexander Joffe has suggested the marks are geometric filler or deliberate gibberish, and that the face looks more like Tiwanaku-era work (c. 200–1000 CE).",
 [('Old World contact (speculative)','Proponents read the marks as a Sumerian text and link the bowl to similar claims about the Pokotia monolith; no specialist in cuneiform has published a confirmed reading, and no other Mesopotamian material is known in the Andes.'),
  ('Mainstream view','Without a secure find context or dating, most archaeologists treat the bowl as an undated local object of uncertain authenticity and the \"inscription\" as non-linguistic.')],
 ['wiki:Fuente_Magna'],'Fuente_Magna',dateNote='Reported find c. 1950; object undated'),
# --- part 2: Central Asia and the Middle East
X('bmac','The Oxus Civilisation (BMAC): a forgotten Bronze Age heartland',H,'confirmed',2,-2300,['archaeological','radiocarbon','ancient DNA'],
 "The Bactria–Margiana Archaeological Complex, or Oxus Civilisation, flourished from about 2300 to 1700 BCE (some say from 2500) in what is now Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan, northern Afghanistan and north-eastern Iran. Excavated mostly from the 1970s by Viktor Sarianidi, sites such as Gonur Depe in the Murghab delta had monumental walled compounds, palaces, temples, irrigation, fine metalwork, stamp seals and elaborate burials, and traded with Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus civilisation. It was largely unknown to the wider world until the late 20th century. Ancient DNA (Narasimhan et al., Science 2019) shows BMAC people descended mainly from Iranian-plateau and Anatolian-related farmers, with little steppe ancestry, while a few individuals show contacts with the Indus world and the steppe.",
 [('Soma/haoma rituals','Sarianidi argued that vessels at Gonur temples held residues of ephedra, poppy or cannabis used to prepare the sacred drink known as soma in the Rigveda and haoma in the Avesta; other researchers dispute the identifications.'),
  ('Indo-Iranian link','Whether BMAC influenced early Indo-Iranian speakers passing through Central Asia (as words and religious ideas suggest to some linguists) is debated; the 2019 genetic data indicate steppe groups mixed in after its height.'),
  ('Wider name','Recent surveys in north-eastern Iran have led some scholars to propose the broader term "Greater Khorasan Civilisation".')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.aat7487','wiki:Bactria–Margiana_Archaeological_Complex','wiki:Gonur_Depe'],'Gonur_Depe',end=-1700,dateNote='c. 2300–1700 BCE (some estimates from 2500 BCE)'),
X('arkaim','Arkaim: a Bronze Age ring-town, and a magnet for Aryan and esoteric myths',H,'confirmed',2,-2100,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "A fortified circular settlement of the Sintashta culture in the steppe of the southern Urals (Chelyabinsk region, Russia), dated to about 2150–1650 BCE (most activity c. 2100–1800). It was found in 1987 by archaeologists surveying an area due to be flooded for a reservoir; the discovery led to the flooding being cancelled and a nature-and-history reserve created. Concentric rings of walls with houses built against them surround a central square; Sintashta sites also produced some of the earliest known spoked-wheel chariots, and are linked by many scholars to early Indo-Iranian speakers.",
 [('Aryan homeland and "Russian Stonehenge" (speculative)','Since the early 1990s Arkaim has been promoted by Russian nationalists, neo-pagans and esoteric groups as the homeland of the "Aryans", the birthplace of Zoroaster, an astronomical observatory or a place of special energies; archaeologists including Victor Shnirelman have documented and criticised these claims, which go well beyond the evidence.'),
  ('Cosmological town plan','Some scholars compare the ring plan with ideal cities in the Rigveda and Avesta; this is an interpretation, not proof of a direct link.')],
 ['doi:10.1163/146481799793648040','wiki:Arkaim','wiki:Sintashta_culture'],'Arkaim',end=-1650,dateNote='c. 2150–1650 BCE; discovered 1987'),
X('issyk-golden-man','The Golden Man of Issyk',H,'confirmed',3,-300,['archaeological','inscription'],
 "In 1969 Kemal Akishev's team excavated a side chamber of the Issyk kurgan, about 50 km east of Almaty in south-eastern Kazakhstan, part of a cemetery of 45 royal mounds. It held the skeleton of a young Saka (eastern Scythian) individual aged 16–18 with about 4,000 gold ornaments: a tall pointed headdress with gold plaques of horses, snow leopards and birds, gold-covered clothing, an iron sword and dagger and a gold-tipped arrow, plus a silver cup with a short inscription in a still undeciphered script. The burial is dated to about the 4th–3rd century BCE (some say earlier). Reconstructed as the \"Golden Man\" on a winged snow leopard, the figure became a national symbol of independent Kazakhstan and crowns the Independence Monument in Almaty.",
 [('Prince or princess?','The skeleton\'s sex has never been firmly established; some researchers have argued the grave goods, including earrings, suit a woman, which would make the national symbol a "Golden Woman" – a question bound up with steppe traditions of armed women.'),
  ('The Issyk inscription','The 26 or so signs on the silver cup have been variously read as an Iranian (Saka) language in a variant of Kharosthi or as an old Turkic script; no reading is accepted.')],
 ['wiki:Issyk_kurgan','wiki:Saka'],'Issyk_kurgan',dateNote='Burial c. 4th–3rd century BCE; excavated 1969'),
X('scythian-amazons','Amazons: Greek myth and the armed women of the steppe',M,'disputed',2,-700,['textual','archaeological','osteological'],
 "In Greek myth the Amazons were a nation of mounted women warriors and archers who fought Heracles, Achilles and Theseus and lived at the edge of the known world, often placed at Themiscyra on the Black Sea coast of Anatolia. Herodotus (5th century BCE) claimed the Sauromatae of the steppes descended from Amazons and Scythians, and that their women rode, hunted and fought. Archaeology has since found many Scythian and Sarmatian graves of women buried with bows, arrows, spears and horse gear, some with battle wounds: by some counts about 20% of warrior graves on the lower Don and Volga, and up to a quarter of Sarmatian military burials, are female. In 2019 Russian archaeologists reported a Scythian burial near Voronezh with armed women of several generations, one wearing a golden headdress.",
 [('Myth rooted in reality','Historians such as Adrienne Mayor argue Greek contact with steppe peoples whose women fought on horseback inspired the Amazon stories.'),
  ('Literary creation','Others stress that the Greek Amazons are largely an inverted mirror of Greek gender norms; armed women in graves do not prove all-female societies, which have no archaeological support.')],
 ['wiki:Amazons','wiki:Sarmatians'],'Amazons',dateNote='Myths attested from c. 8th–7th century BCE; steppe burials c. 7th century BCE–4th century CE'),
X('ulugh-beg','Ulugh Beg\'s observatory: a 40-metre sextant in Samarkand',H,'confirmed',3,1420,['archaeological','textual','astronomical tables'],
 "Ulugh Beg (1394–1449), grandson of Timur and ruler of Samarkand, built a three-storey observatory in the 1420s around a giant meridian arc (the Fakhri sextant) about 40 m in radius, cut partly into a trench in the hill. With astronomers including Jamshid al-Kashi and Ali Qushji he produced the Zij-i Sultani (completed c. 1437), with a catalogue of 1,018 stars – the first major independent star catalogue since Ptolemy – and a value for the sidereal year accurate to within about a minute. After Ulugh Beg was killed in 1449 on the orders of his son, the observatory was destroyed and its location forgotten; archaeologist Vasily Vyatkin rediscovered the sextant trench in 1908.",
 [('Accuracy','Analysis by Verbunt and van Gent (2012) showed most of his star positions were newly measured rather than copied from Ptolemy, and generally more accurate.'),
  ('Science and power','His fate is often told as a clash between science and religious conservatism; historians note dynastic politics and succession struggles were at least as important.')],
 ['doi:10.1051/0004-6361/201219596','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603|UNESCO: Samarkand – Crossroad of Cultures|heritage body','wiki:Ulugh_Beg_Observatory'],'Ulugh_Beg_Observatory',end=1449),
X('tamgaly','Tamgaly petroglyphs and the "sun-headed" figures',H,'confirmed',3,-1400,['rock art','archaeological'],
 "About 5,000 petroglyphs pecked into rock outcrops in a gorge about 170 km north-west of Almaty, Kazakhstan, made from the Bronze Age (mid-2nd millennium BCE) up to the 19th–20th centuries. The most famous Bronze Age carvings show human figures with large round heads ringed by rays and dots, usually read as solar deities, alongside dancers, archers in animal masks, bulls, horses, chariots and birthing women. The site, with nearby altars and burials, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2004.",
 [('Solar deities','Archaeologists interpret the sun-heads as gods or shamans connected with sun worship in Bronze Age steppe religion; similar figures appear at other sites in Kazakhstan and Siberia.'),
  ('Ancient astronauts (speculative)','Popular ancient-astronaut writing presents the rayed heads as helmeted visitors from space; there is no evidence for this, and rayed or haloed heads are a widespread way of depicting divine or solar beings.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1145|UNESCO: Petroglyphs of the Archaeological Landscape of Tanbaly|heritage body','wiki:Tamgaly'],'Tamgaly',dateNote='Oldest carvings mid-2nd millennium BCE'),
X('dead-sea-scrolls','The Dead Sea Scrolls',H,'confirmed',1,-250,['archaeological','textual','radiocarbon','ancient DNA','palaeographic'],
 "From 1946–47, when Bedouin shepherds found jars of scrolls in a cave near Qumran on the north-west shore of the Dead Sea, until 1956, about 15,000 fragments from some 900–1,000 manuscripts were recovered from eleven caves. Written mostly in Hebrew, also Aramaic and Greek, between the 3rd century BCE and the 1st century CE, they include the oldest surviving copies of almost every book of the Hebrew Bible – the Great Isaiah Scroll is about 1,000 years older than the previously oldest known Hebrew manuscripts – plus community rules, hymns, calendars, commentaries and apocalyptic works such as the War Scroll. New science keeps extracting information: DNA from the animal skins (Cell, 2020) helped group fragments, and AI handwriting analysis (PLOS ONE, 2021) suggested the Isaiah Scroll was written by two scribes.",
 [('Who wrote them','The classic view links them to an Essene-like sect living at Qumran; others (e.g. Norman Golb) argue they were libraries brought from Jerusalem and hidden before the Roman destruction of 70 CE; the debate continues.'),
  ('Forgeries','Since 2002 dozens of "new" fragments sold on the antiquities market appeared; in 2020 investigators concluded that all 16 fragments held by the Museum of the Bible in Washington were modern forgeries.'),
  ('Conspiracy claims','Delays in publication into the 1990s fed claims that the scrolls held secrets damaging to Christianity; once fully published they revealed no such content, though they transformed understanding of Second Temple Judaism.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.cell.2020.04.046','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0249769','url:https://www.deadseascrolls.org.il/|Leon Levy Dead Sea Scrolls Digital Library (Israel Antiquities Authority)|archive','wiki:Dead_Sea_Scrolls'],'Qumran_Caves',end=70,dateNote='Written c. 250 BCE–70 CE; found 1946–1956'),
X('copper-scroll','The Copper Scroll: a treasure map from Qumran',H,'disputed',2,70,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Found in 1952 in Qumran Cave 3, the Copper Scroll (3Q15) differs from all the other Dead Sea Scrolls: it is engraved on a sheet of copper (about 1% tin) roughly 2.4 m long, and it is not literature but a list of 64 hiding places of gold, silver, vessels and scrolls – \"in the ruin that is in the valley of Achor, under the steps...\". Too corroded to unroll, it was cut into strips at the Manchester College of Technology in 1955–56. Its Hebrew is closer to that of the Mishnah, and it is dated to around 50–100 CE. The quantities, if read literally, would total many tonnes of precious metal. John Allegro led a search in 1960 and found nothing; no listed cache has ever been identified. It is displayed at the Jordan Museum in Amman.",
 [('Temple treasure','Many scholars think it records real treasures of the Jerusalem Temple hidden before the Roman destruction of 70 CE.'),
  ('Folklore or fiction','Others see it as legend, an exaggerated inventory or a ritual text; the sheer amounts make a literal reading hard to accept.'),
  ('Treasure hunting','Amateur searchers continue to claim to have decoded its locations; none has produced verified finds.')],
 ['doi:10.1163/9789004350267','wiki:Copper_Scroll'],'Copper_Scroll',dateNote='Written c. 50–100 CE; found 1952'),
X('derinkuyu','Derinkuyu: an underground city for 20,000 people',H,'confirmed',2,-700,['archaeological','historical records'],
 "One of over 200 underground settlements carved into the soft volcanic tuff of Cappadocia, central Turkey, Derinkuyu reaches about 85 m deep across many levels, with ventilation shafts, wells, stables, wine and oil presses, chapels and storerooms, and rolling stone doors that could be closed from inside. It is connected to the underground city of Kaymaklı by a tunnel several kilometres long. The first chambers may have been dug by Phrygians around the 8th–7th centuries BCE; Xenophon (c. 400 BCE) described people in Anatolia living in underground houses. It was greatly enlarged in the Byzantine era, especially during Arab–Byzantine wars (c. 780–1180), and used by local Christians as a refuge into the 20th century. Forgotten by outsiders, it was rediscovered in 1963 when a resident knocked through a basement wall during renovations.",
 [('Dating','Because carved rock is hard to date, the earliest phase is uncertain; Phrygian, Hittite and later origins have all been proposed.'),
  ('Ancient catastrophe refuge (speculative)','Some alternative writers suggest the cities were dug much earlier to shelter people from a catastrophe or ice-age conditions; there is no dated evidence for a prehistoric origin.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/357|UNESCO: Göreme National Park and the Rock Sites of Cappadocia|heritage body','wiki:Derinkuyu_underground_city'],'Derinkuyu_underground_city',
 dateNote='Possible origins 8th–7th century BCE; main expansion Byzantine era; rediscovered 1963'),
X('ark-covenant','The Ark of the Covenant: lost, hidden or in Aksum?',M,'myth-legend',1,-950,['textual','religious tradition','oral tradition'],
 "In the Hebrew Bible the Ark of the Covenant was a gold-covered acacia chest made at Mount Sinai to hold the tablets of the law, carried before the Israelites, and later placed in the inner sanctuary of Solomon's Temple in Jerusalem. It disappears from the biblical record; it is not listed among the treasures taken when the Babylonians destroyed the Temple in 587/586 BCE. Jewish traditions say it was taken to Babylon, hidden by King Josiah beneath the Temple, or concealed by Jeremiah in a cave on Mount Nebo. The Ethiopian Orthodox Church holds that it was brought to Ethiopia by Menelik I, son of Solomon and the Queen of Sheba (as told in the Kebra Nagast), and is kept in a chapel at Aksum guarded by a single monk; every Ethiopian church holds a replica tablet (tabot). The Lemba of southern Africa tell of a sacred drum, the ngoma lungundu, as their ark.",
 [('Historical question','Scholars debate whether the Ark existed and what it was; no archaeological trace has been found, and the Aksum object has never been shown to outsiders.'),
  ('Lemba ngoma','In 2007 historian Tudor Parfitt identified a wooden drum-like object in a Harare museum as the Lemba ngoma lungundu, radiocarbon dated to about 1350 CE; it is a revered object in its own right, not the biblical Ark.'),
  ('Fringe theories (speculative)','Claims that the Ark was an electrical device or was found by amateur explorers (e.g. Ron Wyatt) are unsupported.')],
 ['wiki:Ark_of_the_Covenant','wiki:Kebra_Nagast','wiki:Ngoma_lungundu'],'Ark_of_the_Covenant',dateNote='Biblical tradition; absent from records after 587/586 BCE'),
X('durupinar','The Durupınar formation: "Noah\'s Ark" in the hills of Ararat',H,'debunked',3,1959,['geological','aerial photography'],
 "A boat-shaped limonite formation about 164 m long on Mount Tendürek in eastern Turkey, about 29 km south of the summit of Mount Ararat. It drew attention after Turkish army captain İlhan Durupınar noticed it on aerial survey photographs in 1959; Life magazine published it in 1960. American adventurer Ron Wyatt promoted it as the petrified remains of Noah's Ark from 1977, and salvage expert David Fasold, who first supported the idea, later rejected it. Geologist Lorence Collins and Fasold published a study in 1996 concluding it is a natural structure, a view shared by the young-earth creationist geologist Andrew Snelling and by Turkish geologists. Turkey nonetheless created a visitor centre at the site.",
 [('Ark claim','Supporters point to its shape, length close to the biblical 300 cubits, claimed regular iron readings and \"anchor stones\" nearby; these were not confirmed by independent study.'),
  ('Natural formation','Geologists explain the shape as a doubly plunging syncline or mudflow around a rock outcrop; similar shapes occur nearby.')],
 ['doi:10.5408/1089-9995-44.4.439','wiki:Durupınar_site','wiki:Searches_for_Noah\'s_Ark'],'Durupınar_site'),
X('etemenanki','Etemenanki: the real Tower of Babel?',H,'confirmed',2,-600,['archaeological','textual','cuneiform'],
 "Etemenanki (\"Temple of the foundation of heaven and earth\") was the great ziggurat of the god Marduk in Babylon. Its earliest phases are uncertain; it was rebuilt by Nabopolassar and completed by Nebuchadnezzar II in the 6th century BCE, when the Jews were in exile in Babylon. Cuneiform texts give its base as about 91 m square and imply a height of about the same, in seven stages topped by a temple. Herodotus described a tower of eight levels. It was damaged after Persian conquest, and Alexander the Great ordered it cleared for rebuilding, which never happened; little more than the foundation outline survives. A black stone stele in the Schøyen Collection, published by Andrew George in 2011, shows Nebuchadnezzar beside the stepped tower.",
 [('Origin of the Babel story','Many scholars think Genesis 11 – a tower reaching heaven, halted by God confusing human languages – was inspired by Babylon\'s ziggurats, probably Etemenanki, encountered during or before the exile.'),
  ('Older parallel','A Sumerian epic, Enmerkar and the Lord of Aratta, contains a passage in which the god Enki changes or confuses the speech of humanity, which some see as a precursor of the confusion-of-tongues motif; its interpretation is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Etemenanki','wiki:Tower_of_Babel','wiki:Enmerkar_and_the_Lord_of_Aratta'],'Etemenanki',dateNote='Final rebuilding c. 610–560 BCE'),
]
